{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Mono Lake Blooms: Arsenic-Tolerant Purple Bacteria of an Alkaline Soda Lake",
    "date": "2026-08-08",
    "lesson": "Mono Lake, California is a hypersaline, highly alkaline (pH ~9.8) soda lake with naturally high dissolved arsenic. Its meromictic water column stratifies: a thin oxygenated, sunlit surface sits atop a deep anoxic monimolimnion rich in sulfide and arsenate. Purple sulfur bacteria (Ectothiorhodospira-like) exploit the narrow band where residual light still penetrates and sulfide first appears, running anoxygenic photosynthesis (light AND sulfide together). Above them, halotolerant heterotrophs graze organic falls from an Artemia/brine-fly-fed food web, while extreme salinity and alkalinity slow every process down and select hard for osmotic and chemical resistance. The lesson: extremophiles don't just survive harsh chemistry, several groups are metabolically built around it, and a habitat can be simultaneously food-poor, light-poor, and chemically brutal, forcing multiple stresses to overlap in one thin livable layer.",
    "citation": "Oremland et al., Mono Lake microbial ecology and arsenic biogeochemistry studies (1990s-2010s)",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Mono Lake, California — meromictic alkaline soda lake, purple sulfur bacteria at the chemocline, arsenic/sulfide biogeochemistry"
  },
